BUG A: FIXED (one-line change in /gin/errors.go).

ROOT CAUSE
`gin.Error` implements `error` with a **value** receiver (`func (msg Error) Error() string`), so when a user wraps a non-pointer `Error{...}` in `fmt.Errorf("...%w", err)`, the dynamic type stored in the error interface is the value type `gin.Error`. But `Unwrap` was declared with a **pointer** receiver:

    func (msg *Error) Unwrap() error { return msg.Err }

Go method sets: `*Error` has `Unwrap`, but `Error` (the value) does not. `errors.Is`/`errors.As` only look at the method set of the dynamic type, so for the value form the unwrap chain stopped at `gin.Error` and neither the inner error nor the target type was ever seen — both checks silently returned false. The pointer form worked because `*Error` does have `Unwrap`. (Verified the mechanism independently with a minimal repro: value dynamic type → `errors.Is` false, pointer dynamic type → true.)

FIX (exact diff, /gin/errors.go, only change made by me)
@@ -91,7 +91,7 @@ func (msg *Error) IsType(flags ErrorType) bool {
 // Unwrap returns the wrapped error, to allow interoperability with errors.Is(), errors.As() and errors.Unwrap()
-func (msg *Error) Unwrap() error {
+func (msg Error) Unwrap() error {
 	return msg.Err
 }
No other source change, no test file touched, no API/signature change: `*Error` still satisfies `interface{ Unwrap() error }` (pointer method set includes value methods), so all existing pointer-based callers (`errorMsgs` is `[]*Error`, `Context.Error`'s `errors.As(err, &parsedError)`) keep working; the value form now works too. `gofmt` clean, `go vet ./...` clean, `go build ./...` clean.

